@c ============================================================================
@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex folgezettel
@cindex Zettelkasten
@cindex Luhmann

Autoslip Roam is an Emacs package that brings Niklas Luhmann's
folgezettel (follow-up slip) system to org-roam.  The package automatically
manages bidirectional links between parent and child notes based on their
folgezettel addresses.

@section What is a Folgezettel?

A folgezettel is a sequential numbering system used in the Zettelkasten
(slip-box) method.  Each note has an address that encodes its position in
a hierarchy of ideas.  For example:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{1.} - A root note (first topic); the trailing period is the canonical marker for a root
@item
@code{1.2} - Second subtopic of note 1
@item
@code{1.2a} - First branch from 1.2
@item
@code{1.2a3} - Third continuation of 1.2a
@end itemize

Legacy titles without the trailing period on the root (e.g., @code{1 Crystallography}) are still recognized and treated as equivalent to the canonical @code{1.} form.

@section Features

@itemize @bullet
@item
Automatic parent backlink generation when creating child notes
@item
Automatic forward link insertion in parent notes
@item
Bidirectional cross-linking when manually inserting links
@item
Validation to prevent invalid addresses
@item
Duplicate index detection
@item
Suggestions for next child address
@item
Database synchronization for immediate link visibility
@item
Navigation commands to jump to the parent, list direct children, or show
the whole vault as a folgezettel-ordered tree
@item
Reparent commands that move a note (or an entire subtree) to a new
folgezettel address and keep titles, links, and filenames in sync
@item
Chain-of-thought commands that render the path from a note to its root
in a dedicated buffer and can insert that path into the current note
@item
Cross-linked chain-of-thought command that shows the folgezettel
context of every note linked under Cross References
@item
Optional property-drawer link-storage mode for a quieter note body
@item
Transient menu that shows the chain of thought, the neighboring notes,
and the children of the current note at once
@item
Browser dashboard that writes the whole hierarchy to one HTML page
@item
Repair command that adds every missing parent or child link in the vault
@item
Command that links a root note back to the index of indices
@item
Subtree note generator that turns an outline of addressed headlines
into linked notes in one step
@item
Lisp entry point for creating a child note from other packages
@end itemize

@c ============================================================================
@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@section Requirements

@itemize @bullet
@item
Emacs 27.1 or later
@item
org-roam 2.0 or later
@end itemize

@section From MELPA

@findex package-install
Once the package is available on MELPA:

@example
M-x package-install RET autoslip-roam RET
@end example

@section Manual Installation

@enumerate
@item
Clone the repository:
@example
git clone https://github.com/MooersLab/autoslip-roam.git
@end example

@item
Add to your load path in @file{init.el}:
@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/autoslip-roam")
(require 'autoslip-roam)
@end lisp

@item
Enable the mode:
@lisp
(autoslip-roam-mode 1)
@end lisp
@end enumerate

@section Installing the Info Manual

To install the Info manual for access within Emacs:

@example
make info
sudo make install-info
@end example

Or manually:

@example
makeinfo autoslip-roam.texi
sudo cp autoslip-roam.info /usr/local/share/info/
sudo install-info /usr/local/share/info/autoslip-roam.info \
    /usr/local/share/info/dir
@end example

Then access via @kbd{C-h i d m Autoslip Roam RET}.

@c ============================================================================
@node Quick Start
@chapter Quick Start

@section Basic Setup

Add to your @file{init.el}:

@lisp
(require 'autoslip-roam)
(autoslip-roam-mode 1)
@end lisp

@section Creating Your First Notes

@enumerate
@item
Create a root note with a folgezettel title:
@example
M-x org-roam-node-find RET
Title: 1. My First Topic
@end example

@item
Open the root note and create a child:
@example
M-x autoslip-roam-insert-next-child RET
@end example

@item
The package suggests @code{1.1} as the first child.  Enter a title when prompted.

@item
Both notes now have bidirectional links:
@itemize @minus
@item
Child note has ``Parent Note'' section with link to @code{1.}
@item
Parent note has ``Child Notes'' section with link to child
@end itemize
@end enumerate

@c ============================================================================
@node Folgezettel Addresses
@chapter Folgezettel Addresses

@cindex address format
@cindex hierarchy

@section Address Structure

Folgezettel addresses follow a strict hierarchical pattern:

@table @code
@item 1., 2., 3., ...
Root notes (a number followed by a trailing period); bare-integer legacy forms are accepted
@item 1.1, 1.2, 1.13, ...
First-level subtopics (number DOT number)
@item 1.2a, 1.2b, ..., 1.2z
Letter branches (lowercase letters)
@item 1.2aa, 1.2ab, ..., 1.2zz
Extended letter branches (after z comes aa)
@item 1.2a1, 1.2a2, ...
Numeric continuations of letter branches
@end table

@section Alternation Rules

The address system follows strict alternation:

@enumerate
@item
Root numbers can only have DOT-number children (@code{1.} @result{} @code{1.1})
@item
DOT-number nodes can only have letter children (@code{1.1} @result{} @code{1.1a})
@item
Letter-ending nodes can only have number children (@code{1.1a} @result{} @code{1.1a1})
@item
The pattern continues alternating
@end enumerate

@section Parent-Child Relationships

@multitable @columnfractions .3 .3 .4
@headitem Child @tab Parent @tab Relationship
@item @code{1.2} @tab @code{1.} @tab Remove .number, leave root period
@item @code{1.2a} @tab @code{1.2} @tab Remove letters
@item @code{1.2aa} @tab @code{1.2} @tab Remove ALL letters
@item @code{1.2a3} @tab @code{1.2a} @tab Remove trailing number
@item @code{1.2a3b} @tab @code{1.2a3} @tab Remove trailing letters
@end multitable

@section Valid vs Invalid Addresses

@subsection Valid Examples
@itemize @bullet
@item @code{1.} - Root note (canonical); @code{1} is also accepted as a legacy bare form
@item @code{1.13} - Multi-digit subtopic
@item @code{1.2a} - Single letter branch
@item @code{1.2aa} - Extended letter (27th child)
@item @code{1.2a3c5d7} - Deep hierarchy
@end itemize

@subsection Invalid Examples
@itemize @bullet
@item @code{1.2.3} - Multiple periods not allowed
@item @code{1.2A} - Uppercase not allowed
@item @code{1.2a!} - Special characters not allowed
@item @code{a1} - Must start with number
@end itemize

@c ============================================================================
@node Usage
@chapter Usage

@section Automatic Link Creation

@cindex automatic linking
When @code{autoslip-roam-mode} is enabled, creating a new note with
a folgezettel address automatically:

@enumerate
@item
Identifies the parent note based on the address
@item
Inserts a backlink under @samp{** Parent Note} in the new note
@item
Inserts a forward link under @samp{** Child Notes} in the parent
@end enumerate

@section Creating Child Notes

@findex autoslip-roam-insert-next-child
The recommended way to create child notes:

@example
M-x autoslip-roam-insert-next-child
@end example

This command:
@enumerate
@item
Reads the current note's folgezettel address
@item
Suggests the next available child address
@item
Prompts for a title
@item
Creates the note with proper bidirectional links
@end enumerate

@section Manual Link Addition

@findex autoslip-roam-add-backlink-to-parent
For existing notes without links:

@example
M-x autoslip-roam-add-backlink-to-parent
@end example

A link that is already there is left alone, so the command is safe to
run twice.  To repair the whole vault in one step, @pxref{Repairing Links}.

@section Creating Child Notes from Lisp

@findex autoslip-roam-create-child
@cindex Lisp entry point
Other packages can create a child note without prompts:

@lisp
(autoslip-roam-create-child parent-node "Title" address immediate)
@end lisp

@defun autoslip-roam-create-child parent-node title &optional address immediate templates
Create a child note of @var{parent-node} with @var{title} and link the two
notes.  @var{parent-node} is an org-roam node whose title carries a
folgezettel address.  When @var{address} is nil, the next free address is
used.  When @var{immediate} is non-nil, the capture is finished without
showing its buffer, the first template in @var{templates} is used, and the
new node is returned.  @var{templates} defaults to
@code{org-roam-capture-templates}.

The function signals a @code{user-error} when the parent has no address,
when @var{address} is not a valid child of the parent, or when
@var{address} is already in use.
@end defun

The companion package autoslip-noter uses this function to file org-noter
notes on PDF files in the zettelkasten.

@section Cross-Reference Links

@cindex cross-references
When you insert a link to another org-roam note using @kbd{C-c C-l} or
@code{org-roam-node-insert}, a reciprocal link is automatically created
in the target note under @samp{** Cross References}.

This can be disabled:
@lisp
(setq autoslip-roam-auto-crosslink nil)
@end lisp

@c ============================================================================
@node Customization
@chapter Customization

@findex customize-group
All customization options are in the @code{autoslip-roam} group:

@example
M-x customize-group RET autoslip-roam RET
@end example

@section Link Descriptions

@defopt autoslip-roam-parent-link-description
Description text for parent links.  Default: @code{"Parent note"}.
@end defopt

@defopt autoslip-roam-child-link-description
Description for child links.  If @code{nil} (default), uses the child's title.
@end defopt

@section Headings

@defopt autoslip-roam-backlink-heading
Heading for parent links in child notes.  Default: @code{"Parent Note"}.
Set to @code{nil} to insert without a heading.
@end defopt

@defopt autoslip-roam-forward-link-heading
Heading for child links in parent notes.  Default: @code{"Child Notes"}.
@end defopt

@defopt autoslip-roam-crosslink-heading
Heading for cross-reference links.  Default: @code{"Cross References"}.
@end defopt

@section Behavior

@defopt autoslip-roam-auto-crosslink
Enable automatic reciprocal links when inserting cross-references.
Default: @code{t}.
@end defopt

@defopt autoslip-roam-sync-db-before-queries
Sync org-roam database before queries.  Default: @code{t}.
Recommended for reliability.
@end defopt

@defopt autoslip-roam-index-address
Folgezettel address of the note that indexes the root notes.
Default: @code{"00.0"}.  Used by
@code{autoslip-roam-add-backlink-from-root-to-00-index}.
@end defopt

@section Navigation Menu Options

These options are defined in @file{autoslip-roam-nav.el}.

@defopt autoslip-roam-nav-page-size
Maximum number of children shown on one page of the menu.
Default: @code{30}.
@end defopt

@defopt autoslip-roam-nav-title-width
Maximum number of characters shown of a note title in the menu.
Default: @code{70}.
@end defopt

@section Browser Dashboard Options

These options are defined in @file{autoslip-roam-html.el}.

@defopt autoslip-roam-html-file
File that the dashboard is written to.  Default:
@file{autoslip-roam-index.html} in the user Emacs directory.
@end defopt

@defopt autoslip-roam-html-title
Title shown at the top of the dashboard.  Default:
@code{"Autoslip index"}.
@end defopt

@defopt autoslip-roam-html-link-type
How a note in the dashboard links back to the note itself.  With
@code{org-protocol}, the default, a click opens the note in Emacs.  With
@code{file}, the link points to the Org file.  With @code{nil}, the notes
are not links.
@end defopt

@section Link Storage

@defopt autoslip-roam-link-storage
How to record parent and child references.  The value @code{'headings}
(default) writes visible @samp{** Parent Note} and @samp{** Child Notes}
sections in the note body.  The value @code{'properties} writes the
references to a top-level property drawer instead, keeping the body
untouched.  The property-drawer mode is useful in shared vaults where
the visible heading noise creates merge-conflict surface.
@end defopt

@defopt autoslip-roam-parent-property
Property key used for the parent reference when link storage is
@code{'properties}.  Default: @code{"FZ_PARENT"}.
@end defopt

@defopt autoslip-roam-children-property
Property key used for the list of child references when link storage is
@code{'properties}.  Default: @code{"FZ_CHILDREN"}.
@end defopt

@section Reparenting

@defopt autoslip-roam-rename-files-on-reparent
Whether @code{autoslip-roam-reparent} and
@code{autoslip-roam-reparent-subtree} should rename each affected
file on disk so that the filename prefix matches the new folgezettel
address.  Default: @code{t}.
@end defopt

@section Chain of Thought

@defopt autoslip-roam-chain-heading
Second-level heading written above a chain-of-thought outline that is
inserted into a note.  Default: @code{"Chain of Thought"}.  Set to
@code{nil} to insert without a heading.
@end defopt

@defopt autoslip-roam-chain-crosslink-heading
Heading written above cross-linked chains-of-thought inserted into a
note.  Default: @code{"Cross-linked Chains of Thought"}.
@end defopt

@section Example Configuration

@lisp
(use-package autoslip-roam
  :after org-roam
  :config
  (setq autoslip-roam-parent-link-description "↑ Parent"
        autoslip-roam-backlink-heading "Parent Note"
        autoslip-roam-forward-link-heading "Child Notes"
        autoslip-roam-auto-crosslink t)
  (autoslip-roam-mode 1))
@end lisp

@c ============================================================================
@node Commands
@chapter Commands

@section Interactive Commands

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-mode
Toggle the minor mode.  When enabled, automatically creates bidirectional
links for new notes with folgezettel addresses.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-insert-next-child
Create a new child note of the current note.  Suggests the next available
address and prompts for a title.  Creates bidirectional links automatically.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-add-backlink-to-parent
Manually add bidirectional links between the current note and its parent.
Useful for existing notes or when automatic linking fails.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-repair-links
Find and add the parent and child links that are missing in the vault.
With a prefix argument, report only.  @xref{Repairing Links}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-add-backlink-from-root-to-00-index
Link the current root note back to the index of indices.  With a prefix
argument, do this for every root note.  @xref{Repairing Links}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-report-validation-errors address
Interactively validate an address and display any errors.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-diagnose-address address
Diagnose issues finding a folgezettel address in the database.
Shows all nodes and their extracted addresses.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-check-duplicate-index address
Check if an address is already used.  Warns if duplicate found.
@end deffn

@section Navigation Commands

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-goto-parent
Visit the parent of the current note.  The parent is the node whose
folgezettel address is one step shallower than the current one.  Errors
when the current note is a root.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-list-children
Prompt with a completion list of the current note's direct children,
ordered by folgezettel, and visit the selection.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-show-tree
Open the @file{*Autoslip Tree*} buffer, a read-only outline of every
folgezettel-addressed node in the vault, sorted in canonical folgezettel
order and indented by depth.  In that buffer, @kbd{RET} visits the node on
the current line, @kbd{n} and @kbd{p} move between lines, @kbd{g} refreshes
the view, and @kbd{q} buries the buffer.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-nav
Walk the hierarchy in a transient menu.  With a prefix argument, start at
the root notes.  Defined in @file{autoslip-roam-nav.el}.
@xref{Navigation Menu}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-html-open
Write the browser dashboard and open it in the web browser.  Defined in
@file{autoslip-roam-html.el}.  @xref{Browser Dashboard}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-html-export
Write the browser dashboard without opening it.  With a prefix argument,
ask for the file.
@end deffn

@section Chain-of-Thought Commands

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-show-chain-of-thought
Open the @file{*Autoslip Chain of Thought*} buffer, which lists every
ancestor of the current note from root to leaf along a single folgezettel
chain.  The view shows only the connecting notes (not the rest of the
subtree).  In that buffer, @kbd{RET} visits the node on the current line,
@kbd{i} inserts the chain into the note that opened the buffer, @kbd{g}
refreshes, @kbd{n} and @kbd{p} move between lines, and @kbd{q} buries the
buffer.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-insert-chain-of-thought
Insert the current note's chain of thought at point as an indented org
bullet list.  Each bullet is a link to the corresponding org-roam node,
and the heading above the list is controlled by
@code{autoslip-roam-chain-heading}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-show-crosslinked-chains
Open the @file{*Autoslip Cross-linked Chains*} buffer.  For every note
linked under @code{autoslip-roam-crosslink-heading} in the current
note, the command walks that note's own folgezettel chain and renders it
as a distinct block in the buffer.  This gives the reader the context each
cross-link sits in rather than just the link's title.  Press @kbd{i} in
the buffer to insert all the chains into the current note.
@end deffn

@section Reparenting Commands

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-reparent new-address
Change the folgezettel address of the current note to
@var{new-address}.  The command validates the new address, rejects
duplicates, updates the title keyword, rewrites the parent backlink, and
updates the old and new parents' forward-link sections.  When
@code{autoslip-roam-rename-files-on-reparent} is non-nil, it also
renames the file on disk.
@end deffn

@deffn Command autoslip-roam-reparent-subtree new-address
Like @code{autoslip-roam-reparent}, but also moves every descendant
of the current note so that their addresses all shift to live under
@var{new-address}.  The command pre-checks for address collisions with
unrelated notes and aborts without making changes if any are found.
@end deffn

@section Suggested Key Bindings

@lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'org-roam
  (define-key org-mode-map (kbd "C-c n c") #'autoslip-roam-insert-next-child)
  (define-key org-mode-map (kbd "C-c n p") #'autoslip-roam-add-backlink-to-parent)
  (define-key org-mode-map (kbd "C-c n u") #'autoslip-roam-goto-parent)
  (define-key org-mode-map (kbd "C-c n d") #'autoslip-roam-list-children)
  (define-key org-mode-map (kbd "C-c n t") #'autoslip-roam-show-tree)
  (define-key org-mode-map (kbd "C-c n h") #'autoslip-roam-show-chain-of-thought)
  (define-key org-mode-map (kbd "C-c n H") #'autoslip-roam-insert-chain-of-thought)
  (define-key org-mode-map (kbd "C-c n x") #'autoslip-roam-show-crosslinked-chains)
  (define-key org-mode-map (kbd "C-c n r") #'autoslip-roam-reparent)
  (define-key org-mode-map (kbd "C-c n R") #'autoslip-roam-reparent-subtree))

;; The menu and the dashboard are useful from any buffer.
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c n v") #'autoslip-roam-nav)
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c n w") #'autoslip-roam-html-open)
@end lisp

@c ============================================================================
@node Navigation Menu
@chapter Navigation Menu

@cindex transient menu
@cindex navigation menu
@findex autoslip-roam-nav

The command @code{autoslip-roam-nav} walks the folgezettel hierarchy in a
transient menu.  The menu redraws itself at each step, so you can move
through a chain of notes without opening a file until you want to.
Following links between pairs of files shows one note at a time.  The
menu shows the chain above the current note, its neighbors, and its
children at once.

The menu is in the optional file @file{autoslip-roam-nav.el}, which needs
Emacs 28.1 or later and transient 0.4.0 or later.

@lisp
(require 'autoslip-roam-nav)
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c n v") #'autoslip-roam-nav)
@end lisp

@kbd{C-c n v} starts at the note at point when it has an address.
@kbd{C-u C-c n v} starts at the root notes.

@ifnottex
@ifnotinfo
@image{images/autoslip-roam-nav-menu,,,The transient navigation menu,png}
@end ifnotinfo
@end ifnottex

@section What the Menu Shows

For a note such as @code{1.2b}, the menu looks like this:

@example
Chain of thought
1. Crystallography
  1.2 Phasing
    1.2b Experimental phasing
 RET Visit this note
 SPC Peek at this note
 ^ Up to 1.2 Phasing
 < Previous: 1.2a Molecular replacement
 > Next: 1.2c Direct methods

Children (2)
 1 1.2b1 SAD
 2 1.2b2 MAD

Menu
 ~ Roots    % Orphans (2)    @@ Jump to any note    + New child    ! Refresh
@end example

@section Keys

@table @kbd
@item @var{child key}
Move to that child.
@item RET
Visit the current note and close the menu.
@item SPC
Show the current note in another window and keep the menu open.
@item ^
Move up to the parent, or to the root notes.
@item <
@itemx >
Move to the previous or next sibling.
@item ~
Move to the root notes.
@item %
List the orphans.
@item @@
Jump to any note by name in the minibuffer.
@item +
Visit the current note and create a child of it.
@item [
@itemx ]
Previous or next page when a note has many children.
@item !
Read the notes again.
@item C-g
Close the menu.
@end table

@section Root Notes and Orphans

@cindex root note
@cindex orphan
The first screen lists only root notes.  A root note has a title that
begins with an integer and a period, such as @samp{1. Crystallography}.
A title such as @samp{001 Writing of papers} or @samp{Untitled 3} is not
a root in the menu, although the package can read a number from it.  The
list is computed from the org-roam database, so no hand-kept index is
needed.

A note that is not a root and whose parent does not exist is an orphan.
Orphans are kept off the first screen and have a screen of their own on
the @kbd{%} key.  That screen doubles as a list of broken chains.

@section Keys of the Children

The key of a child is the end of its address: the child @code{1.2c} has
the key @kbd{c}, and the child @code{1.2a7} has the key @kbd{7}.  When
one key would be the start of another, the keys are padded to one width.
Under the root @code{1.}, the children @code{1.1}, @code{1.2}, and
@code{1.14} get the keys @kbd{0 1}, @kbd{0 2}, and @kbd{1 4}.  The fixed
commands use punctuation, so they never collide with the keys of the
children.

@c ============================================================================
@node Browser Dashboard
@chapter Browser Dashboard

@cindex dashboard
@cindex HTML
@findex autoslip-roam-html-open
@findex autoslip-roam-html-export

The command @code{autoslip-roam-html-open} writes the whole hierarchy to
one HTML page and opens it in the web browser.  The page is in the
optional file @file{autoslip-roam-html.el}, which also loads
@file{autoslip-roam-nav.el}.

@lisp
(require 'autoslip-roam-html)
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c n w") #'autoslip-roam-html-open)
@end lisp

@ifnottex
@ifnotinfo
@image{images/autoslip-roam-html-dashboard,,,The browser dashboard,png}
@end ifnotinfo
@end ifnottex

@section Features of the Page

@itemize @bullet
@item
Only the root notes show at the start.  Each root unfolds level by level.
@item
The number beside a note is the count of all notes below it.
@item
A filter box narrows the tree to the matching notes and unfolds the
chains that lead to them.  Press @kbd{/} to start and @kbd{Esc} to clear.
@item
Two buttons unfold or fold the whole tree.
@item
The page remembers which notes you unfolded, so it looks the same after
you write it again.
@item
Notes without a parent note are listed in a section at the bottom.
@item
The page is one file with no outside resources.  It works without a
network connection, and it prints with all chains unfolded.
@end itemize

The page is a snapshot.  Run the command again to bring it up to date.
Roots and orphans follow the same rules as in the navigation menu.

@section Links Back to Emacs

@cindex org-protocol
With the default value of @code{autoslip-roam-html-link-type}, each note
is an @samp{org-protocol://roam-node} link that opens the note in Emacs.
This needs three things: @code{(require 'org-roam-protocol)} in your init
file, a running Emacs server, and an operating system that sends
org-protocol links to @command{emacsclient}.  On macOS the last item
needs a small helper application.  When that is not set up, use one of
the other values:

@lisp
(setq autoslip-roam-html-link-type 'file) ; link to the Org files
(setq autoslip-roam-html-link-type nil)   ; a page without links
@end lisp

@c ============================================================================
@node Subtree Note Generator
@chapter Subtree Note Generator

@cindex subtree note generator
@cindex outline
@cindex headlines, notes from
@findex autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-preview
@findex autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate
@findex autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate-from-file
@findex autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate-from-clipboard

The command @code{autoslip-roam-insert-next-child} creates one child
note at a time.  The subtree note generator creates a whole branch at
once.  You write the branch as an outline of addressed headlines, and
one command turns each headline into a note with its parent and child
links in place.  Think of the outline as the blueprint and of the
generator as the builder.

The generator is in the optional file
@file{autoslip-subtree-notes-generator.el}.

@lisp
(require 'autoslip-subtree-notes-generator)
@end lisp

@section Tutorial

This tutorial builds a small branch below the root note
@samp{7. Crystallography}.  Try it in a test vault first.

@enumerate
@item
Write the outline in the parent note.  Visit the parent note and add one
headline per planned note.  Each headline is stars, a folgezettel
address, and a title.

@example
:PROPERTIES:
:ID:       0d4c6a52-6c1b-4a8e-9a3e-2f5d7c1b9e10
:END:
#+title: 7. Crystallography

* Outline
** 7.1 Crystal growth
*** 7.1a Vapor diffusion
*** 7.1b Screening strategies
** 7.2 Symmetry and space groups
*** 7.2a Screw axes
@end example

The address decides the parent of each note.  The number of stars does
not, so the outline can start at any level.

@item
Preview the plan with @kbd{M-x autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-preview}.
Press @kbd{RET} at the prompt for the kinds of notes to accept
@samp{plain}.  The @file{*Autoslip Subtree Notes*} buffer lists the
notes that would be written.  Nothing is written at this step.

@example
Plan: 5 notes below 7.
Outline from this note

7.1            plain      7_1_crystal_growth.org
7.1a           plain      7_1a_vapor_diffusion.org
7.1b           plain      7_1b_screening_strategies.org
7.2            plain      7_2_symmetry_and_space_groups.org
7.2a           plain      7_2a_screw_axes.org
@end example

@item
Generate the notes with @kbd{M-x autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate}.
The command shows the same plan and asks one question.  Answer @kbd{y}
to write the notes.  The file names follow the parent note.  When its
file name starts with an org-roam timestamp, the new file names do too.

@item
Look at the result.  Each new note has a new ID, the headline as its
title, ten blank lines for your text, and a link back to its parent.  A
note with children of its own also lists them.

@example
:PROPERTIES:
:ID:       8542a59c-1c8d-48d6-b1fe-21edd4fd8449
:END:
#+title: 7.1 Crystal growth
@end example

@noindent
Ten blank lines follow the title, and then:

@example
* Parent Note
[[id:0d4c6a52-6c1b-4a8e-9a3e-2f5d7c1b9e10][Parent note]]

* Child Notes
[[id:3fa0db8c-96b2-487c-ae5c-cd5f38565974][7.1a Vapor diffusion]]
[[id:5b0e7c39-2c55-4d0b-8a57-0f3f1f6f2a11][7.1b Screening strategies]]
@end example

The parent note gains a @samp{Child Notes} section with links to
@samp{7.1} and @samp{7.2}.  Its outline is left as it is.  The new notes
are in the org-roam database at once.

@item
Grow the branch later.  Add more headlines to the outline and run the
command again.  A note whose address is already in the vault is never
overwritten.  It is skipped, so only the new headlines become notes.
@end enumerate

@section Outline in a File or in the Clipboard

@cindex clipboard
@cindex outline file
The outline does not have to sit in the parent note.  In every case, run
the command from the buffer of the parent note, because the new notes
are linked to that note.

@table @code
@item autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate
Read the outline from the parent note.  With @kbd{C-u}, ask where the
outline is.
@item autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate-from-file
Read the outline from a file.  The file is only read and is never
changed.  It does not have to be an org-roam note.
@item autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate-from-clipboard
Read the outline from the system clipboard, or from the newest entry in
the kill ring when the clipboard is empty.
@item autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-preview
Show the plan and write nothing.  With @kbd{C-u}, ask where the outline
is.
@end table

@section Kinds of Notes and Templates

@cindex kinds of notes
@cindex templates
@vindex autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-kinds
@vindex autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-templates-directory
The commands ask for the kinds of notes, in level order and separated by
commas.  The first kind is for the children of the parent note, the
second for the grandchildren, and the last kind repeats for all deeper
levels.  With @samp{hub,plain}, the notes @samp{7.1} and @samp{7.2} are
hub notes and everything below them is plain.  The default answer comes
from @code{autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-kinds}.

The kind @samp{plain} needs no template.  Any other kind needs a
template file with the name of the kind in
@code{autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-templates-directory}.  A template
@file{hub.org} could look like this:

@example
#+filetags: :hub:
#+date: $@{date@}
* Overview of $@{heading@}
* Open questions
@end example

The keyword lines of the template go directly below the title, above the
ten blank lines.  The headings of the template go below the
@samp{Parent Note} section.  A template must not hold a @samp{#+title:}
line or a property drawer.  These placeholders are filled in:

@table @code
@item $@{title@}
The address and the headline.
@item $@{heading@}
The headline without the address.
@item $@{address@}
The folgezettel address.
@item $@{kind@}
The kind of the note.
@item $@{date@}
The date of today.
@end table

@section Options

@table @code
@item autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-kinds
Default kinds of notes in level order.  Default: @code{("plain")}

@item autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-templates-directory
Directory of the template files.  Default: @code{nil}

@item autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-blank-lines
@vindex autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-blank-lines
Number of blank lines between the title block and the parent heading.
Default: @code{10}

@item autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-copy-index-lines
@vindex autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-copy-index-lines
Whether to copy the lines that start with @samp{#+LATEX: \index@{} and
sit directly below a headline to the note.  Default: @code{t}
@end table

@section Rules and Limits

@itemize @bullet
@item
Only headlines whose address lies below the address of the parent note
become notes.  Other headlines are ignored, and so are headlines with an
invalid address such as @samp{7.2.3}.
@item
Every new note needs a parent note in the outline or in the vault.  A
headline such as @samp{7.5a} without a note or a headline for
@samp{7.5} stops the command before anything is written.
@item
An address that appears twice in the outline stops the command.
@item
The parent note needs an ID.  The command offers to create one when it
is missing.
@item
Only the @code{headings} value of @code{autoslip-roam-link-storage} is
supported.
@item
Back up or commit your vault before the first run.
@end itemize

@section Use from Lisp

The generate function takes the kinds, a flag to skip the question, a
flag for a dry run, and the outline source.

@lisp
;; Outline in the current note, no question.
(autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate '("hub" "plain") t)
;; Outline in a file, or in the clipboard.
(autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate '("plain") t nil "~/outline.org")
(autoslip-subtree-notes-generator-generate '("plain") t nil 'clipboard)
@end lisp

@c ============================================================================
@node Repairing Links
@chapter Repairing Links

@cindex repair
@cindex missing links
@findex autoslip-roam-repair-links

A vault that was built before automatic linking, or edited by hand, can
have notes without their parent or child links.

@example
C-u M-x autoslip-roam-repair-links    report only
M-x autoslip-roam-repair-links        report, ask once, then repair
@end example

The command compares every note that has an address with its parent note
and lists what is missing in the @file{*Autoslip Repair*} buffer.  A line
that starts with @samp{B} is a note without its link to the parent.  A
line that starts with @samp{F} is a parent without its link to the note.
Nothing is changed until you answer the one question.  Links that are
already there are left alone, so the command is safe to run more than
once.  Notes whose parent does not exist are skipped, because there is
nothing to link them to.  Back up or commit your vault before the first
repair.

@section Linking Root Notes to the Index

@cindex index of indices
@findex autoslip-roam-add-backlink-from-root-to-00-index

A root note has no parent, so it has no parent link.  To link a root note
back to the index of indices instead, use:

@example
M-x autoslip-roam-add-backlink-from-root-to-00-index
C-u M-x autoslip-roam-add-backlink-from-root-to-00-index
@end example

The first form links the current root note.  The second form links every
root note.  The index of indices is the note whose title carries the
address in @code{autoslip-roam-index-address}, @code{"00.0"} by default.
The link is placed where a parent link goes and shows the title of the
index note.  Only the root note is changed.

